{"rules":[{"jurisdiction":"NJ","level":"state","category":"algorithmic_rent_setting","status":"not_yet_effective","title":"Forbidding the Algorithmic Inflation of Rent (FAIR) Act","requirement":"Makes it unlawful, and a violation of the New Jersey Antitrust Act, for a rental property owner (or its agent, representative or subcontractor) to pay or exchange consideration for the services of a coordinator. It is also unlawful for a coordinator to facilitate a tacit or express agreement among rental property owners that restricts competition, including by performing a coordinating function. Two or more persons may not engage in or facilitate parallel pricing coordination, agents or representatives of a coordinator may not engage in it, and no person may perform a coordinating function. A coordinating function includes collecting competitively sensitive information of two or more owners to feed an algorithm that sets or recommends rents, lease terms or occupancy levels. It also includes setting rents using an algorithm that processes other owners' competitively sensitive information, and setting or recommending the same or a substantially similar algorithmic output to two or more owners. Exclusions: research or testing not used for pricing, free public rent estimates, equal-terms real estate brokerage databases that do not set or recommend prices, and government affordability controls. The Attorney General must set up a complaint location and may adopt rules.","key_value":"Ban on using algorithmic coordinators and on parallel pricing coordination for residential rentals","coverage_conditions":"Applies to all residential dwelling units (houses, apartments, accessory units or other units intended as a primary residence) in the State, and to rental property owners and coordinators.","exemptions":"Residential dwelling unit excludes inpatient medical care, licensed long-term care, and detention or correctional facilities. Government entities setting or limiting rents through affordability controls are not coordinators. Spreadsheets operating without AI and requiring human analysis, and databases that only use an algorithm to query unprocessed data, are not algorithmic devices.","coverage_logic":{"covers":null,"exempt":null},"subject":"landlord","interaction":"Municipalities are prohibited from enacting ordinances that conflict with the act, except ordinances explicitly authorized or required by other law. The act is in addition to, and does not authorize conduct already unlawful under, the New Jersey Antitrust Act or other law.","yields_to_local":false,"preempts_local":true,"effective_date":"2027-07-01","effective_rule":{"type":"first_day_of_nth_month_after","n":12,"enacted":"2026-07-20"},"enacted_date":"2026-07-20","end_date":null,"penalty":"Violations are violations of the New Jersey Antitrust Act, and sections 6 through 17 of P.L.1970, c.73 (C.56:9-6 through 9-17) continue to apply to violations. The document states no specific amounts.","citation":"P.L.2026, c.43","quoted_span":"a coordinator to facilitate a tacit or express agreement among rental property owners that restricts competition with respect to residential dwelling units, including by performing a coordinating function;","supporting_spans":["A municipality shall be prohibited from enacting an ordinance that conflicts with this act.","This act shall take effect on the first day of the twelfth month next following the date of enactment."],"confidence":0.93,"conflict_note":null}],"notes":"Single in-scope law. The effective date is computed as the first day of the 12th month after enactment on 2026-07-20, which gives 2027-07-01, so the status is not_yet_effective as of 2026-10-01. The municipal conflict-preemption clause is folded into this record as preempts_local rather than recorded as a rent control prohibition."}
